<p:notes xmlns:a="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/main" xmlns:p="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/presentationml/2006/main" xmlns:r="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships" xmlns:p14="http://schemas.microsoft.com/office/powerpoint/2010/main" xmlns:p15="http://schemas.microsoft.com/office/powerpoint/2012/main" xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"><p:cSld><p:spTree><p:nvGrpSpPr>        <p:cNvPr id="1" name=""/>        <p:cNvGrpSpPr/>        <p:nvPr/>      </p:nvGrpSpPr>      <p:grpSpPr>        <a:xfrm>          <a:off x="0" y="0"/>          <a:ext cx="0" cy="0"/>          <a:chOff x="0" y="0"/>          <a:chExt cx="0" cy="0"/>        </a:xfrm>      </p:grpSpPr><p:sp><p:nvSpPr><p:cNvPr id="188" name="PlaceHolder 1"></p:cNvPr><p:cNvSpPr><a:spLocks noGrp="1"/></p:cNvSpPr><p:nvPr><p:ph type="sldImg"/></p:nvPr></p:nvSpPr><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="685800" y="1143000"/><a:ext cx="5486040" cy="3085920"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:ln w="0"><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:sp><p:sp><p:nvSpPr><p:cNvPr id="189" name="PlaceHolder 2"></p:cNvPr><p:cNvSpPr><a:spLocks noGrp="1"/></p:cNvSpPr><p:nvPr><p:ph type="body"/></p:nvPr></p:nvSpPr><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="685800" y="4400640"/><a:ext cx="5486040" cy="3600000"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln w="0"><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr><p:txBody><a:bodyPr lIns="91440" tIns="45720" rIns="91440" bIns="45720" anchor="t"><a:noAutofit/></a:bodyPr><a:p><a:pPr indent="0" algn="l"><a:buNone/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2000" b="0" u="none" strike="noStrike"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="000000"/></a:solidFill><a:effectLst></a:effectLst><a:uFillTx/><a:latin typeface="Arial"/></a:rPr><a:t>The library story is broad enough that different teams will care about different items. JSON Lines serialization can simplify log pipelines, streaming exports, and datasets where each line contains a complete JSON value. Full outer joins remove a recurring LINQ workaround when unmatched rows from both inputs must be preserved. Preview 7 added HTTP request compression and configurable connection eviction, which can matter for clients that send large payloads or need tighter connection-lifecycle control. New DNS record-resolution APIs expose more than a simple address lookup. Decimal floating-point types and generic Complex support expand the numerics toolbox, although teams must still verify precision, interoperability, and persistence semantics for their domain. ZIP APIs gained password and metadata capabilities. None of these features transforms every application, and that is precisely the point. A mature standard library grows by absorbing common, error-prone infrastructure code. During upgrade planning, teams should search for home-grown helpers that a supported framework API can safely replace.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2000" b="0" u="none" strike="noStrike"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="000000"/></a:solidFill><a:effectLst></a:effectLst><a:uFillTx/><a:latin typeface="Arial"/></a:endParaRPr></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="0" algn="l"><a:buNone/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2000" b="0" u="none" strike="noStrike"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="000000"/></a:solidFill><a:effectLst></a:effectLst><a:uFillTx/><a:latin typeface="Arial"/></a:rPr><a:t>Sources:</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2000" b="0" u="none" strike="noStrike"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="000000"/></a:solidFill><a:effectLst></a:effectLst><a:uFillTx/><a:latin typeface="Arial"/></a:endParaRPr></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="0" algn="l"><a:buNone/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2000" b="0" u="none" strike="noStrike"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="000000"/></a:solidFill><a:effectLst></a:effectLst><a:uFillTx/><a:latin typeface="Arial"/></a:rPr><a:t>https://devblogs.microsoft.com/dotnet/dotnet-11-preview-5/</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2000" b="0" u="none" strike="noStrike"><a